  "narrative": "The current accepted markers are MSI-high, TP53 loss, KEAP1 loss, and PTEN loss, all internally robust to some mutual co-occurrence and the dominant Avana library. The next highest-value science is not merely to retest these markers, but to test whether additional measured predictors contribute beyond the large accepted signals and whether the accepted effects are context-dependent. I therefore defer several lower-priority pending lineage-specific checks and the PTEN-PIK3CA main contrast: lineage subtype did not show a main effect, the accepted marker effects are already large and robust across other backgrounds, and the registered PIK3CA interaction H32 is a more direct context test than H23. For new proposals, I test CDKN2A loss and RB1 loss as checkpoint/cell-cycle predictors expected to associate with weaker measured dependency, i.e. higher composite scores, while conditioning on TP53-loss-negative, KEAP1-loss-negative, and MSI-negative cells. This addresses the plausible alternative that any checkpoint-loss association is carried by co-occurring TP53 or KEAP1 loss. I also test ARID1A loss and SMARCA4 loss as chromatin-remodeling predictors expected to create stronger measured dependency, i.e. more negative composite scores, in the same accepted-marker-free stratum. This tests the alternative that chromatin remodeling loss marks a vulnerability axis rather than the checkpoint axis captured by TP53/KEAP1. Finally, I test a TP53-loss interaction with screen_batch in batch_a, because a large measured association could be amplified by technical batch structure. The anticipated direction for the batch interaction is uncertain; a near-zero interaction would support robustness across batches, whereas a threshold-crossing interaction would require reinterpreting TP53-loss as batch-sensitive. Direction convention remains as before: for direction -1 claims, a positive signed estimate means stronger dependency in the exposed group; for direction +1 claims, a positive signed estimate means higher/less-negative composite scores, i.e. weaker measured dependency. All effects remain supplied-dataset associations unless validated and not causal claims.",
  "research_notes": "Accepted supplied-dataset associations: MSI_high -> stronger dependency (-1) main +0.775, robust TP53-intact/TP53-KEAP1-intact/Avana; TP53_loss -> weaker/higher composite (+1) main +0.829, robust MSI-neg/KEAP1-intact/Avana; KEAP1_loss -> weaker/higher composite (+1 reversal) main +0.810, robust TP53-intact/TP53-MSI-clean/Avana but pending independent validation; PTEN_loss -> stronger dependency (-1) main +0.239, independent validation +0.294, moderate robust TP53-intact/KEAP1-intact/Avana. Negative/closed: lineage subtype main, KRAS, BRAF, MTAP, STK11 main, MYC, marker_e; EGFR stronger-dependency direction rejected with only subthreshold opposite tendency. Current priority set: run interactions H29-H32 for accepted markers, marker D/F main checks H20-H21, STK11 conditional in KEAP1-intact cells H36, new conditional checkpoint markers CDKN2A/RB1 in TP53/KEAP1/MSI-intact cells, new chromatin markers ARID1A/SMARCA4 in same stratum, and TP53-loss batch interaction. Deferrals: H16-H18 lineage-specific accepted-marker checks and H23 PTEN PIK3CA main contrast are deferred, not closed, to prioritize context interactions and new predictors. If new markers exceed threshold, follow up with PTEN/other accepted co-occurrence checks and possible validation. If batch interaction is positive, inspect other accepted markers by batch. All conclusions remain dataset-level associations only."
